    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Pressure solution seams (PSSs) are closing mode structures of localised dissolution resulting in a volume reduction. We present new observations constraining the processes by which PSSs initiate and grow in low porosity clastic rocks from southern Ireland. PSSs initiate as inter-granular PSSs at quartz grain to grain contacts. As quartz dissolves, clay remains as a residue along the contacts as well as filling the adjacent pores to form incipient PSSs involving multiple grain boundaries. Further lengthening and thickening occurs by lateral and transverse linkage of neighbouring segments of incipient PSSs respectively. Multiple PSS segments are observed to concentrate in thin tabular zones that appear as single macroscopic PSSs visible in hand samples. The length and spacing of PSSs have log-normal distributions at outcrop, hand sample and thin section scale.\r\n\r\nA finite element model is used to investigate the growth of PSSs, which are idealised as localised volume reduction structures (LVRSs). The numerical analyses show that significant compressive stresses are concentrated in the tip areas and around asperities on the flanks of single LVRSs. A creep law is also implemented to simulate LVRS growth. Using the calculated normal compressive stresses and volumetric plastic strains as proxy for LVRS growth, our numerical models of PSSs show the ability of both lateral and transverse growth, which confirms our laboratory and field observations.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
